1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 { In this chapter we will focus on 4.Bc4 but we will also have a look at some lesser known moves, even 4.a3 and 4.a4. } 4. Bc4 { Black should be happy to see this move, which gives him an opportunity to immediately take over the initiative with a typical tactic. -KEY- } Nxe4 $1 { This trick was known in the 19th century. For example in 1886 it was featured in the game Dirk Van Foreest - Arnold Van Foreest (yes, these are the ancestors of Lucas and Jorden!). } 5. Bxf7+ $2 { This primitive move backfires quickly. } Kxf7 6. Nxe4 d5 7. Neg5+ Kg8 8. d4 h6 { Just pushing the knight to the rim. } 9. Nh3 Bg4 $17 { White's position is a sorry sight. Black's king despite making some moves is completely safe, we have a bishop pair, great control in the centre and we can even think about ...g5 and ...Rh7. } 
